=== Pedagogical 10-Frame Example ===
Frame | Ref (Hz) | Test (Hz) | Status                | Rel Err (%) | Cents     | Gross?
------+----------+-----------+-----------------------+-------------+-----------+-------
   0  |      0.0 |       0.0 | Both unvoiced (UV)    |           - |         - | No
   1  |    120.0 |     120.0 | Both voiced (V)       |       0.00% |     +0.00 | No (fine)
   2  |    150.0 |     152.0 | Both voiced (V)       |       1.33% |    +22.93 | No (fine)
   3  |    200.0 |     196.0 | Both voiced (V)       |       2.00% |    -34.98 | No (fine)
   4  |    220.0 |     224.0 | Both voiced (V)       |       1.82% |    +31.19 | No (fine)
   5  |      0.0 |     130.0 | Voicing error (V/UV)  |           - |         - | VDE
   6  |    140.0 |       0.0 | Voicing error (V/UV)  |           - |         - | VDE
   7  |    100.0 |     200.0 | Both voiced (V)       |     100.00% |  +1200.00 | YES (GPE)
   8  |    300.0 |     150.0 | Both voiced (V)       |      50.00% |  -1200.00 | YES (GPE)
   9  |      0.0 |       0.0 | Both unvoiced (UV)    |           - |         - | No

--- Summary Breakdown ---
Total frames: N = 10
Voiced in Ref: 7, Voiced in Test: 7
Both voiced frames: N_both = 6 (Frames 1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 8)
Voicing errors (VDE): 2 frames (Frames 5, 6) -> VDE = 2/10 = 20.0%
Gross Pitch Errors (GPE): 2 frames (Frames 7, 8) out of 6 both-voiced frames -> GPE = 2/6 = 33.3%
F0 Frame Errors (FFE): VDE + GPE = 2 + 2 = 4 -> FFE = 4/10 = 40.0%
Fine cents (excluding GPE frames 7 and 8, on frames 1, 2, 3, 4):
  Frame 1:  +0.00 cent
  Frame 2: +22.93 cent
  Frame 3: -34.98 cent
  Frame 4: +31.19 cent
  Mean absolute cents: 22.28 cent
  Std of cents: 25.64 cent
  95th percentile: 34.41 cent
  Max fine cent error: 34.98 cent

Explanation of 20% threshold:
20% frequency ratio: 1200 * log2(1.20) = 315.64 cents = 3.16 semitones.
Why exclude gross errors from fine-cent statistics?
An octave error (2x) is 1200 cents! If included, a single octave error would distort the mean cent error from 22 cents to hundreds of cents, hiding the true precision of the fine interpolation algorithm.
